**Saved:** `docs/content-analysis/perspective-reviews/Marcus-Aurelius/2026-08-27_020004/subject.md`

**Verdict:** trust `strained` · value `high` · delight `clear_hit` · recommendation `revise` — **3 blockers, 9 concerns**

**Strongest hit** — the empathy turn that refuses to absolve: the Book 1 debt-ledger explanation of why he couldn't rest, immediately followed by *"That still made him hard to be around, and Fronto was right to push."* Most sympathetic profiles stop one sentence early. This one concedes the cost to the people around him instead of converting the explanation into a defense. Alongside it: the article omits the Faustina sex rumors and says in the FAQ that it's omitting them.

**Highest-priority finding (SUBJECT-R1)** — the closing beat inverts the passage it rests on, and the same cut is made twice. The article ends by saying Marcus's self-verdict "never revised," citing Book 8's opening. I verified the full Long text: 8.1 exists to remove "the desire of empty fame" and turns at *"throw away the thought, How thou shalt seem to others, and be content."* The other self-criticism quote does the same thing — 5.9's "return back again" continues *"and be content if the greater part of what thou doest is consistent with man's nature."* Both quotations are severed at the exact clause where he stops accusing and starts absolving. That's a selection rule, not a slip, and it fires three paragraphs after the article correctly indicts the Stoicism industry for clipping him onto posters.

The other two blockers are attribution: the "philosophical old woman" slur is assembled from two HA documents thirteen years apart and dated to a revolt it can't belong to (it's inside a letter attributed to Lucius Verus, dead by 169), and the five-days-of-mourning quote stops immediately before the clause where the source explains itself — the games of Jupiter were in progress.

Two notes on scope: all three blockers are repairable with text from sources the draft already cites, and two of the repairs make the piece stronger rather than safer. The highest-value *addition* isn't a blocker at all — it's SUBJECT-C9, that written self-examination is a prescribed Stoic exercise, so doing it isn't by itself diagnostic. The packet calls that the strongest objection available to a skeptic; the page never faces it.
